-- title:
--   $\zeta'/\zeta$ is holomorphic on any set avoiding $s=1$ and the zeros of $\zeta$
-- statement:
--   Let $S\subseteq\mathbb{C}$ be a set such that $1\notin S$ and $\zeta(s)\ne 0$ for every $s\in S$. Then the logarithmic derivative of the Riemann zeta function is holomorphic on $S$:
--
--   $$s \longmapsto \frac{\zeta'(s)}{\zeta(s)} \quad\text{is holomorphic on } S.$$
--
--   The hypotheses are exactly what is needed: away from $s=1$ the zeta function and its derivative are both differentiable (the completed function $(s-1)\zeta(s)$ is entire), and the nonvanishing of $\zeta$ on $S$ makes the quotient well behaved.
--
--   This small general-purpose lemma packages the differentiability bookkeeping for $\zeta'/\zeta$ once and for all; the concrete holomorphy statements on rectangles used in the Prime Number Theorem contour argument (both the tall zero-free-region rectangles and the fixed low rectangle) are obtained by combining it with a zero-free-region hypothesis for the relevant set.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/AlexKontorovich/PrimeNumberTheoremAnd/blob/f55e85551ac10e96d98262a354cfcaac2825f2da/PrimeNumberTheoremAnd/ZetaBounds.lean#L2678-L2685

